<!--
  A conforming RGB HDR Display profile per clause 8.10, carrying HDR Image and
  HDR Display metadata but no HAGC tag.

  This is the shape NOTE 3 of 8.10.1 describes: "the presence of a conforming
  cicpTag is the sole tag-level requirement that distinguishes an HDR Profile
  from a conventional three-component matrix-based Input or Display profile".
  Every tone-mapping descriptor of 8.10.3 is optional, so a profile with none
  of them is still a conforming HDR Profile, and the tone-mapping operator is
  the consuming CMM's to supply (8.10.2 b).

  The metadataTag exercises the display-headroom precedence of 8.10.5. All
  three HDR Display entries are present and they deliberately DISAGREE:

    DERH = 4.0
    DCV  max luminance 1000, DRWL 203   ->  1000/203 = 4.926

  NOTE 13 makes that disagreement the point rather than a mistake: "When all
  three entries are present but disagree, the priority list above (a to d) is
  authoritative: DERH wins, and the derived values from DCV/DRWL are not
  recomputed." A reader that averages, cross-checks, or prefers the derived
  value returns 4.926 here; a correct one returns 4.0 and reports that rule a)
  produced it.

  ENTRY SHAPES: the four HDR Image entries follow the ICC dictType Metadata
  Registry (HDR Image category, registered 2025-06-04). CRWL is one floating
  point number; CLL is maximum and average light level then an 8-bit ITU-T
  H.273 primaries code; MDCV is maximum and minimum luminance then the same
  primaries code. DERH, DCV and DRWL are not registered - clause 8.10.5 is not
  yet accepted - so they are written as 8.10.5 describes them, with DCV on the
  shape of its registered sibling MDCV. 9 is BT.2020, 1 is BT.709.
-->
